    {
      "voter": "$brock",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://brock.iagon.net/votes/gov_action1m3xx08yv788vfxqh6nfvrjtvmqpwezsy0ggaczctkyjmttc2wmxsq4jsr7q.jsonld -->\r\n# $brock\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Net Change Limit (Epoch 613 to Epoch 713)\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytjyvm958ywjkp57f8wm3havj72lc653tp7ajttxxt6ftgcmcmdk2`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThis is once again a massively excessive NCL. I was right about the last NCL being way too high and causing public caution against buying the Ada token. The NCL is a public declaration of how much Ada can and most likely will be sold over the time period. It is not just an \"upper limit\" to spending as some suggest, it is effectively a spending target. So long as there is money on the table, people will propose ways to spend it, and as we witnessed last year that Ada will get spent. With a higher ceiling, every proposal is bound to be inflated to take advantage of how much Ada is available. We have seen nearly no returns on our last year's excessive spending beyond core development, and nothing suggests that there will be any change to that in the next year. We must tighten our belt and be much more demanding in our expectations of treasury-funded ventures. We can not spend millions of Ada fronting the bill for conference events and parties that do not pay back the treasury after profiting on ticket prices, all while the Ada token sinks in value. We can not be funding un-proven development teams to build products with no demand. It all starts with the NCL. And beyond the size of the NCL being excessive, the time period is also just absurd. We need to stick to calendar-year timeframes. Throwing in strange timeframes such as 16-month terms appears as an attempt to obfuscate and complicate the tracking of spending data over time. It makes us look both dishonest and amateurish.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "$Khale",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/Qmbmi6cjJECLki3rxSsUwTXQG8ZMWWPKZnu7hNMEnL3adj -->\r\n# $Khale\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Net Change Limit (Epoch 613 to Epoch 713)\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2ltat8kjqrmnff3lkkpxy0j5tn66d3m0gy64dc92asft5g6dl9ws`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI support the need for a Net Change Limit for Epochs 613–713, but I do not support setting it at 350M ADA on the basis of the analysis provided. The proposed cap is chosen top‑down from past inflows and the previous NCL (≈306.9M ADA net income uplifted to 350M ≈115%), largely to accommodate an undrawn 50M DeFi Liquidity Budget, without a bottom‑up view of category‑level forecasts, a treasury sustainability trajectory, or ROI‑style reporting on how the last NCL’s spending actually performed. This falls short of responsible, program‑driven budgeting.\r\n\r\nIn practice, the prior 350M NCL has already been treated as a soft spending target, with an extension passed once the cap was approached, rather than as a rigorously justified ceiling. Re‑adopting the same headline number, again without clear forward planning or outcome analysis, risks normalizing that behavior. I am therefore voting NO on this 350M ADA NCL and would prefer a future NCL that is set at a more conservative level and explicitly grounded in transparent forecasts and retrospective impact reporting.",
      "vote": "NO"
