      "voter": "intertreeJK",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmXUt1mW6j24BdAkbSo6n2Egb9DHNEYUK29vgwu3vMQ6Qp -->\r\n# intertreeJK\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfkg5ryqwaleunwqg0dtcj8g8yw8sgd9kskg2zlzdr8355gpghcvv`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAt first I had a concern about what effect reducing the treasuryCut (tau) by half would have on the future setting of Nett Change Limits Year by Year. As reducing the amount that is coming *into* the Treasury may necessitate a reduction on how much goes back *out* of the Treasury each year.\r\n\r\nFurther thinking had me start considering that this may actually be a *good* thing, though.\r\n\r\nAs I am a fan of the argument that reducing the replenishment rate of the Treasury helps to force a more fiscally conservative approach to the Budgetary Policy of Cardano in the future.\r\n\r\nThe 2nd consideration I had of this Proposal is that it will increase the Staking Rewards going to Delegators by a stated 12.5% above current ROA percentages of each Pool.\r\n\r\nThis is indeed a nett positive and will help, in at least the shorter term, to encourage people to continue to Delegate to Pools and keep the Security Level of the overall Cardano Network as high as it can be.\r\n\r\nI say ‘in the shorter term’ as the Rewards Rate will, of course, continue to decrease as the Reserves Level decreases, so this increase is more of a ‘kicking a can down the road’ positive effect, but still a good one.\r\n\r\nTransaction Volume will ultimately be the protector of the Rewards Rate to Delegators. \r\n\r\nWell, Transaction Volume AND potentially alternative revenue streams from things like Partner Chains, but this is outside the scope of this Rationale.\r\n\r\nThe final consideration is that Parameter Changes like this one are fully reservable, and if any genuine economic issues are identified as a result of halving the Treasury Cut, a further Governance Action can be posted later On-Chain to reverse the negative effects that came out of Ratifying this Proposal.\r\n\r\nGiven these 3 considerations, I am voting in support of this change of the tau Parameter to a value of 0.1. Knowing that reversal in the future is possible if needed, and because I genuinely consider the outcomes of this change to at least potentially be good. \r\n\r\nWhether or not the outcomes ARE good will only be seen by allowing the change to take place. So I do not wish to make a stand against this change, in the interest of being able to actually gather data following such a change to help see if it was, in fact, good.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Jakub Szyca",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jakubszyca/Cardano-Drep/refs/heads/main/Raw-Votes-Context/Vote_Context_2.jsonld -->\r\n# Jakub Szyca\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytee8ue66qdqkfquq5c9hld7trhxvqdra5plfhyuragcfsgrqen4n`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI believe this proposal has no merit. A potential 0.5% increase in staking rewards is unlikely to be a game changer for anyone, whereas a 50% reduction in treasury income would have significant long-term consequences. I see no compelling justification for this change.\r\n\r\nOne of the main arguments in the proposal is that lowering the \"tax\" will lead to ADA price appreciation. In reality, the opposite is more likely. A higher treasury cut effectively removes more ADA from circulation, reducing supply in the open market. With stable or increasing demand, this scarcity supports price growth. Lowering the cut would have the opposite effect.\r\n\r\nMoreover, a smaller treasury cut is unlikely to meaningfully impact incentives for developers or users, but it would undoubtedly weaken the long-term stability of the treasury, which is a critical component of our ecosystem. Additionally, the argument that a smaller treasury would encourage more competitiveness and thoughtful spending is not grounded in reality. Poor budget management can occur regardless of budget size—the real determining factor is the people overseeing it. It is our responsibility to ensure proper management, and quite frankly, reducing the treasury would only make it harder for projects to secure funding. This, in turn, could discourage developer engagement in our ecosystem.\r\n\r\nFor these reasons, I see no valid justification for this proposal, and I believe the 20% rate should remain unchanged for the foreseeable future.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "JNTHN_BRGS",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/JonathanBRGS/DRep/refs/heads/main/ID_AG_941502b0aa104c85%2B%2B%2B.jsonld -->\r\n# JNTHN_BRGS\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2yppwm9hvwdnh38fznaug4qy86zfvsmsrqlyvdqk22s2xgmqn42k`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWe are against the cut, because the proposed change is sudden and drastic. If the community deems the cuts necessary, it is preferable that they be small and gradual - if more intense changes are to be made - so that the market can appreciate the network, finding the greatest possible predictability.\r\n\r\nAlthough lower treasury rates promote - as alleged - higher dividends, they also promote a greater volume of currency in circulation - which promotes depreciation of the asset. Therefore, since we do not find any technical or operational benefits, we see the proposal as purely speculative.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "jonahkoch",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jonahkoch/DRep/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context_Reduce_tau.jsonld -->\r\n# jonahkoch\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yt8trnz8e5cnylmyygt0pyzrfw92rt9s42kg8t4rhm6t0dstyvmdn`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am voting no not because I disagree with the concept, but because I believe at this point in the development cycle of the ecosystem it is not appropriate.  I would prefer an incremental approach that would be less disruptive, and the Protocol Parameter Governance Action Type as proposed does not fully comply with the requirements put forward in the Cardano Constitution.\r\n\r\nA healthy treasury is needed for network stability and future growth of the ecosystem.  These early stages of ecosystem development, will require accelerated treasury spending, so keeping expansion at the current rate maintains ecosystem stability.  It is essential to understand the ecosystem requirements before making changes to treasury funding. \r\n\r\nThe negative impact on defi activities and TVL growth will degrade Cardano's market share to competitive ecosystems with more active and lucrative defi markets.  Additionally defi activities are the primary driver of all transaction volume in the ecosystem.  Reducing transaction volume will have a negative knock on effect to treasury growth degrading ecosystem stability.  Furthermore, defi yields need to be more competitive than staking rewards to attract liquidity provisions to and within the ecosystem.\r\n\r\nWhat about Basho?  I do not consider the Cardano roadmap completed until we have addressed the Basho era.  There are several scaling enhancements that need to be fully developed and implemented, until we know the costs or have them completed we need to maintain a healthy and growing treasury.\r\n\r\nWhen the ADA crypto currency can hold a $1 USD floor for more than 12 months I would consider that a sufficient fiat valuation threshold to justify a reduction in tau as it relates to price in fiat.\r\n\r\nThe Cardano network is still bootstrapping and has not fully developed its' roadmap, and why it still requires significant ecosystem adoption before it can safely reduce the current admittedly high tau for treasury funding.",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Josh Marchand",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yHSJ/cardano-votes/refs/heads/main/941502/Vote_Context.jsonld -->\r\n# Josh Marchand\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yt49c9gfsrhf3uzl0ayw0rwykhqssmc6akyhwa7lyvuhltgehnnhv`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am voting NO on this proposal. An increase in APY of ~0.5 (~4% to ~4.5%) will not have a noticeable benefit for the vast majority of ADA holders. However, it will significantly reduce treasury income. The proposal's rationale relies on \"higher ADA prices.\" While all the reasoning follows given higher ADA prices, a government should not assume best possible outcomes. Cardano governance must remain resilient especially as price fluctuates down.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, it is too early in the governance process to begin to restrict the treasury funds. I believe we should be frugal in our treasury spending, but I do not believe we should be restrictive. Since the proposed constitution (still being voted on) prohibits changes to the `treasuryCut` more often than once ever 6 months, we should wait to consider reducing the treasuryCut until we've already gone through at least one treasury withdrawal and seen the result. If as a community we are very aggressive with spending, it would be a mistake to reduce our income. If we are very frugal and efficient, it is worth reconsidering this proposal.\r\n\r\nIn conclusion, I do not believe that this proposal will have a significant increase in network security through staking, and I disagree with the premise that we should consider the value of our treasury at higher than current ADA prices. Additionally, I think that it would be a mistake to restrict our treasury income before this government has demonstrated it's style of spending those funds.",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Kaizen Crypto",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/KaizenCrypto/Drep/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context.jsonld -->\r\n# Kaizen Crypto\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygm547mhyuh70yjt2ewg078dc33jcrcym9pwf6svyj37enq8jqzqa`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nContext\r\nThe Cardano treasury tax, defined by the \"tau\" (τ) parameter, dictates the percentage of staking rewards and transaction fees allocated to the treasury each epoch. Currently set at 20%, this tax has built a substantial treasury, exceeding 1.5 billion ADA as of early 2025. The treasury funds ecosystem development, including protocol upgrades and community initiatives like Project Catalyst. The proposal to reduce this rate to 10% stems from ongoing debates within the Cardano community about balancing network sustainability with staking incentives.\r\n\r\nAs a Cardano Drep, it is my responsibility to uphold the values underlying the Cardano ecosystem and vote in the best interests of the community. I have outlined below several key points that can help explain my rationale.\r\n\r\nPros of Voting \"Yes\" (Reduce to 10%)\r\nIncreased Staking Rewards: Reducing the tax from 20% to 10% would redirect a larger share of epoch rewards to stake pool operators (SPOs) and delegators.\r\nEnhanced Network Participation: Higher rewards could attract more SPOs and delegators, potentially boosting decentralization.\r\nImproved Liquidity and ADA Appeal: With more ADA circulating among stakers rather than accumulating in the treasury, liquidity could rise. This might enhance ADA’s attractiveness to investors, potentially supporting price stability or growth.\r\nAlignment with Maturity: Some community members argue that Cardano’s treasury, currently over 1.5 billion ADA, and the network's maturity allow for a lower tax. This aligns with a shift toward self-sustainability, relying more on transaction fees than reserves.\r\n\r\nCons of Voting \"Yes\" (Reduce to 10%)\r\nReduced Ecosystem Funding: Halving the tax could cut monthly treasury inflows by approximately 15 million ADA (based on current estimates of 30 million ADA added monthly). This could limit funding for critical upgrades and community projects, potentially slowing Cardano’s competitiveness.\r\nLong-Term Sustainability Risks: Some might argue that a reduced tax undermines the treasury’s ability to support Cardano’s future, especially as reserve-based rewards decline over time. With a capped supply of 45 billion ADA, transaction fees will eventually dominate treasury income, and a lower tax now might strain that transition.\r\nLack of Data: Posts opposing the change highlight insufficient evidence to justify halving the tax. Without clear projections on its impact on staking rates or ADA price, the move risks destabilizing the economic model, as noted by several community members.\r\nPotential Whale Influence: A smaller treasury might shift funding power to wealthy stakeholders, reducing the democratic intent of Project Catalyst and favoring \"whales\" over smaller holders.\r\n\r\nPros of Voting \"No\" (Maintain 20%)\r\nSustained Development: Keeping the tax at 20% ensures robust funding for Cardano’s future development.\r\nEconomic Stability: A larger treasury buffers against market volatility and reserve depletion, as noted in Cardano Foundation blogs. This stability could reassure investors and developers, supporting ADA’s valuation indirectly.\r\nCommunity Empowerment: A well-funded treasury sustains initiatives like Project Catalyst, which has allocated over $32 million to nearly 940 projects. This fosters grassroots innovation, benefiting all holders.\r\n\r\nCons of Voting \"No\" (Maintain 20%)\r\nLower Staking Incentives: Retaining the 20% tax keeps staking rewards lower, potentially discouraging participation.\r\nPerceived Overfunding: With over 1.5 billion ADA already amassed, some argue the treasury is bloated, and maintaining a high tax unnecessarily hoards resources that could benefit stakers.\r\nCompetitive Disadvantage: Comparisons to Ethereum suggest Cardano’s flexible staking could gain an edge with a tax cut, making ADA more appealing versus rivals with locked staking models.\r\nRationale for a \"Yes\" Vote and Benefits for Cardano Holders\r\n\r\nVoting \"Yes\" to reduce the treasury tax from 20% to 10% aligns with Cardano’s evolution into a mature, community-driven blockchain. The current treasury, exceeding 1.5 billion ADA, provides a strong foundation, amassed over years of 20% taxation. Halving the tax now acknowledges this success while shifting focus to rewarding active participants—SPOs and delegators—who secure the network. This increases staking rewards, likely boosting participation and decentralization, which are core to Cardano’s proof-of-stake model. For holders, this means higher returns on staked ADA, enhancing its utility and appeal as an investment. Additionally, redirecting ADA to circulation could improve liquidity, potentially stabilizing or lifting ADA’s price. While funding concerns exist, the treasury’s size and future transaction fee reliance suggest Cardano can sustain development with a leaner tax rate.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Ken Ladd",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cardanoesq/15MAR2025-DecreaseTreasuryTax-ym0d7/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context.jsonld -->\r\n# Ken Ladd\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygkfv2u7aazrfhqgh0qssramjd6av09rsh3ejd0rd95cmusezderl`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nDRep Voting Rationale: Protocol Parameter Change to reduce treasuryCut from 20% to 10%\r\n\r\nCriteria\r\n\r\nI assess proposed governance actions in light of the Constitution and my DRep objectives. Because I assess this proposal does not meet requirements of the former, I do not proceed to apply the latter in depth. The burden of proof lies with the proposer of an action, and I assess that which they submit in the action.\r\n\r\nDecision\r\n\r\nFor the reasons explored below — insufficient rationale to justify the requested change and lack of sufficient technical review and scrutiny — I vote no on this action.\r\n\r\nI seek to provide constructive criticism, and these critiques are not personal — they are offered for broader consideration as we explore governance action drafting as a community.\r\n\r\nI thank the proposer(s) for endeavoring to advance the cited goals of economic sustainability and long-term ecosystem growth, and I remain open to considering future proposals on this topic.\r\n\r\nThe Proposal Does Not Sufficiently Justify The Requested Change (Art. III, § 5, ¶ 1)\r\n\r\nConstruction\r\n\r\nArt. III, § 5, ¶ 1, provides two interrelated requirements for proposed governance actions:\r\n\r\n“Sufficient rationale shall be provided to justify the requested change to the Cardano Blockchain. The rationale shall include, at a minimum, a title, abstract, reason for the proposal, and relevant supporting materials.”\r\n\r\nTo apply these two requirements, I must first distinguish necessary from sufficient conditions. A necessary condition must be satisfied to result in an outcome, but its satisfaction does not guarantee that outcome. In comparison, a sufficient condition, if satisfied, guarantees that outcome. \r\n\r\nHere, a rationale with “at a minimum, a title, abstract, reason for the proposal, and relevant supporting materials” is a necessary — but not sufficient — condition to satisfy the “sufficient rationale…to justify the requested change to the Cardano Blockchain” requirement.\r\n\r\nCritically, I must avoid the fallacy of affirming the consequent — improperly concluding the “sufficient rationale” requirement is met because: 1.) a “sufficient rationale” requires “a title, abstract, reason for the proposal, and relevant supporting materials,” and 2.) those minimum elements are present here.\r\n\r\nThus, while this proposal includes “a title, abstract, reason for the proposal, and relevant supporting materials,” the question of whether the rationale justifies the proposed change to the Cardano Blockchain remains, which I assess next. \r\n\r\nBarriers to the Rationale Sufficiently Justifying the Requested Change\r\n\r\nAssertions without sufficient substantiation\r\n\r\nFor a governance action rationale to sufficiently justify the requested change to the Cardano Blockchain, assertions must be supported by data, research, facts, logical arguments, or some other form of evidence.  \r\nOne assertion in this action — that implementation would increase staking incentives — includes relevant evidence in the Mathematical Justification section. There, the change is integrated with a relevant overview of the treasuryCut parameter and Cardano’s staking rewards system.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, many other assertions of the proposal are not supported by sufficient data, research, facts, logical arguments, or some other form of evidence. This isn't to say these assertions cannot be proved, only that the rationale as submitted does not sufficiently do so.\r\n\r\nExamples include unsubstantiated assertions that implementing the action would: improve decentralization, increase security, align economic sustainability with the Cardano ecosystem's long term growth, ensure a balanced economic model that incentivizes participation and network security, encourage broader participation, strengthen security while maintaining a sustainable treasury, result in a leaner treasury encouraging competitive grant proposals and more strategic allocation of resources, and others.\r\n\r\nAs a result, the proposal has recurring instances of circular reasoning where the proposal assumes its own unproven assertions as evidence for its conclusions. For example, the assertion “This proposal should be justified by Cardano's staking participation rates, treasury utilization, and historical transaction volume” (Justification Based on Cardano’s Economic Landscape section) begs the question — assuming its own unproven assertions as evidence for its conclusion. Sufficient data, research, facts, logical arguments, or some other form of evidence must establish how and why staking participation rates, treasury utilization, and historical transaction volume justify the requested change. \r\n\r\nAssertions susceptible to misinterpretation - arbitrary initial settings\r\n\r\nIn three sections of the proposal — Motivation, Referencing Constitutional Guardrails, and Benefits to the Ecosystem — a common pattern of assertions is offered: 1.) that “the initial 20% treasury cut was set arbitrarily at the launch of Shelley and has never been reassessed,” 2.) that Cardano is more mature now, and 3.) insufficiently supported assertions about positive effects of the proposal. \r\n\r\nThis pattern of assertions could be interpreted to infer a rule not found in the Constitution — that decreased levels of justification and scrutiny apply to Protocol Parameter Change actions if the initial value is asserted to have been set arbitrarily. Notwithstanding conflicts with Art. III, § 5, ¶ 3 and other requirements, adopting this rule would only exacerbate the very problem implicit to its premise — that setting parameter values without sufficient justification is adverse to the interests of Cardano. The Referencing Constitutional Guardrails section asserts the initial setting was “not based on economic modeling or sustainability projections but rather as a starting point in the early phases of the network.” This is offered in contrast to our “more mature ecosystem,” which in turn supports the conclusion that we should justify parameter changes utilizing such evidence, not present in this proposal. \r\n\r\nAssertions susceptible to misinterpretation - guardrail bounds\r\n\r\nThe proposal’s Constitutional Alignment section, referencing Appendix I, Section 2.2, TC-01 and TC-02, asserts “The new constitution explicitly permits treasury cuts between 10% and 30%,” then notes “This governance action remains within those limits.”\r\n\r\nIt’s critical to note that compliance with the delimited bounds of pertinent guardrail values is a necessary — not sufficient — condition for proposal justification. Protocol Parameter Change actions must comply with applicable guardrails, provide substantive justification for the specific change being proposed (Art. III, § 5, ¶ 1), undergo sufficient technical review and scrutiny (Art. III, § 5, ¶ 3), and comply with other Constitutional requirements.\r\n\r\nIf guardrail range compliance was treated as sufficient justification, consistent application would lead to untenable results. As Appendix I notes, “Not all parameters for the Cardano Blockchain can be considered independently. Some parameters interact with other settings in an intrinsic way. Where known, these interactions are addressed in this Appendix.” For Economic Parameters, including treasuryCut, “Changes to the economic parameters should not be made in isolation…They need to account for external economic factors (and) network security concerns” (Appendix I, § 2.2). Because not all combinations of intrinsic parameter contraindications are known, let alone in conjunction with the compounding effects of extrinsic conditions, each proposed Protocol Parameter Change action should be evaluated individually based on intrinsic and extrinsic conditions then in existence pursuant to the requirements of Art. 3, § 5, ¶¶ 1-3.\r\n\r\nLack of methodology and narrow scope of consideration\r\n\r\nThe action proposes a reduction of the treasuryCut parameter from 0.2 (20%) to 0.1 (10%). Unfortunately, it omits the methodology and reasoning underlying the chosen value. While the Ensuring Continuity and Preventing Disruptions section posits a beneficial idea — that a “structured review mechanism can be introduced to assess (the parameter change) impact over time and make future adjustments if needed” — the possibility of implementing such measures later does not justify the proposal’s lack of prospective impact analysis now. \r\n\r\nOverall, the proposal focuses primarily on reiterating asserted yet unsubstantiated benefits of the change. This narrow scope leaves consideration of first and following-order effects on the treasury — critical to long-term viability — insufficiently explored. No opportunity cost analysis compares the utility of the pertinent funds through each channel, staking rewards versus treasury utilization, and corresponding identification and possible mitigation of drawbacks are absent.\r\n\r\nThe Proposal Lacks Sufficient Technical Review and Scrutiny (Art. III, § 5, ¶ 3)\r\n\r\nUnder Article III, § 5, ¶ 3:\r\n\r\n“...Protocol Parameter Change governance actions shall undergo sufficient technical review and scrutiny as mandated by the Guardrails to ensure that the governance action does not endanger the security, functionality, performance or long-term sustainability of the Cardano Blockchain. \r\n\r\nUnder Appendix I, § 2.2, changes to Economic Parameters including treasuryCut, “ should not be made in isolation…They need to account for external economic factors and network security concerns.”\r\n\r\nWhile the proposal asserts in the Review and Governance Compliance section that it “incorporates economic impact analysis”, this is not apparent. If this references the Referencing Constitutional Guardrails section, the analysis narrowly posits treasury viability as a function of undefined ADA price appreciation — an unsupported assumption that excludes alternative possibilities of stagnation, decrease, or increases below the undefined increase the argumentation assumes.\r\n\r\nDRep Ken Ladd: drep1ygkfv2u7aazrfhqgh0qssramjd6av09rsh3ejd0rd95cmusezderl",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Kevin G Mohr",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kronoshus/k/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context.jsonld -->\r\n# Kevin G Mohr\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytwn2u7qpy4ur7k3xqd7eehft3g2yjpxf46rugwzmndkyesd0m5nt`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nReducing the treasury cut from 20% to 10% directly benefits Cardano’s staking ecosystem by increasing staking rewards by approximately 12.5%. This increase enhances the attractiveness of staking and actually makes staking a worthwhile economic model for small projects trying to get off the ground in Cardano. This in turn could help many projects struggling right now sustain themselves.\r\n\r\nOne of the biggest flaws with Catalyst right now is projects have to continually propose stuff to get money from them to sustain their businesses. We need an alternative to Catalyst and this proposal is a first step towards doing such a thing. The \"Send to the treasury, withdraw from Catalyst lottery\" does not work and is not good for long term sustainability.\r\n\r\nI believe the proposal is constitutional and in line with the spirit of decentralization. I also will vote yes to this proposal in protest against the arbitrary requirements set forth in the Cardano Constitution.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Kit Willow",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmQ4B3MeVXF5zMUXwwfFfG34WD7yzc1zyHkCu2vKN5E224 -->\r\n# Kit Willow\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygk7qax0ztrv7a7unzgs4apj5mhk5a0058ne3fgfftj4tmgvuk5a7`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI voted NO because reducing the tax significantly impacts the replenishment of the treasury negatively.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhile it is true that deserving and very hardworking SPOs will earn more, however, I believe the Cardano Blockchain ecosystem right now needs sufficient replenishment to the treasury to be able to make the necessary investment needed for growth and advancement. At this point, I believe reducing treasury tax by half will significantly inhibit that, thus my rationale for voting NO. \r\n\r\nIt’s possible in the future that we may be in a better position to implement this ti the ease of every ADA holder, SPO and community member.",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Kostas Panagias",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kostaspanagias/drep/refs/heads/main/voting/2025/2/taxratecut.jsonld -->\r\n# Kostas Panagias\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygc29nv90qqtf7qwlp5eqwnscdmvk5utnul9arfm9eklw9cphfhhu`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n\r\nMy vote for the proposal to decrease the treasury tax from 20% to 10% is **NO**. Below is my rationale.\r\n\r\n**Github Link of this file**: https://github.com/kostaspanagias/drep/blob/main/voting/2025/2/taxratecut.md – You can visit this link to view the formatted version of these contents. The GitHub page provides a more user-friendly and human-readable format compared to the on-chain or Cardano Explorer metadata.\r\n\r\n## Rationale\r\n\r\n* **It's easier to reduce the tax rate than to increase it.** If we reduce the tax rate now to the lowest permissible amount and later find that the treasury is depleting faster than anticipated, it would be more challenging to increase it again. \r\n\r\n* **The proposed decrease is too rapid.** I would prefer a more conservative approach, where we gradually lower the tax rate (e.g., from 20% to 18%, then to 15%, etc.), evaluate the results at each step, and proceed cautiously with further adjustments.\r\n\r\n* **The impact on staking rewards is minimal.** Current staking rewards are below 4%, with a more realistic range of 2.5%–3.5%. A 12% increase in rewards would bring them to approximately 2.8%–3.9% (a net increase of just 0.3%–0.4%). **I fail to see how such a small increase would attract users from other blockchains** to join Cardano solely for staking rewards.\r\n\r\n* **There is insufficient research supporting this move.** To my knowledge, no research, paper, or detailed analysis has been presented to demonstrate the potential impact of this tax reduction or simulate how it might deplete treasury reserves over time. Without proper data, **it's difficult to assess whether this change would have a minor or significant impact on the treasury.**\r\n\r\n* **We need substantial funds for development and marketing.** Blockchain technology is still in its early stages, and I anticipate that we will require significant resources for development (e.g., scaling solutions, quantum resistance technologies) and marketing (e.g., driving adoption, improving communication, educating users). A higher treasury tax ensures a steady flow of funds to support these critical needs.\r\n\r\n* **This is our first year managing community funds.** As a community, we are in the early stages of learning how to govern ourselves and allocate resources effectively. Inefficiencies, errors, and potential misallocations are inevitable as we refine our processes through trial and error. This is not about wastefulness or bad actors – it’s simply part of the learning curve.\r\n\r\n* **Startups reinvest profits; so should we.** Drawing a parallel with startups: In their early stages, startups typically reinvest profits rather than distributing dividends until they establish themselves within their markets and industry lifecycle. Similarly, Cardano should prioritize reinvesting transaction-generated funds into growth rather than increasing \"dividends\" (i.e., staking rewards). These investments will position us as a leading blockchain in the future, where an **increased number of transactions** and price appreciation will more than compensate for our early investments.\r\n\r\n* **Increased staking rewards do not encourage network usage.** Even a small percentage increase in rewards at this stage promotes inertia rather than active participation in the ecosystem. Cardano offers exciting opportunities for participants to engage meaningfully: earning yield in decentralized finance (DeFi), participating in DAOs, trading NFTs, engaging with memecoin communities, using decentralized exchanges, and exploring real-world assets and decentralized applications (dApps). Active participation helps users understand blockchain's potential, educates the community, attracts new users, and drives adoption. This increased engagement will boost network utility and ensure sustainability – both in terms of transactions generating tax revenue and actual usage.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "KryptoLabs",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kenerik/KryptoLabs/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context%209%20mars.jsonld -->\r\n# KryptoLabs\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfeqm8wfd2h9pf2c9y934qskuw3z04selrpr7esf4gdsl2c5zvpgf`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nGiven the potential risks to long-term sustainability, we are voting no on the proposal to decrease the treasury tax from 20% to 10%.\r\nMaintaining the current rate ensures that the treasury can continue to adequately fund necessary activities,\r\nsupport future growth, and maintain reserves for unexpected needs.\r\nThis approach safeguards Cardano's ability to remain competitive and resilient, fulfilling its commitment to a sustainable blockchain ecosystem.",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "LALKUL",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Juana-Attieh/lalkul/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context.jsonld -->\r\n# LALKUL\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytnjaqmwq9qk9yv2a5ff79qj2r2mgmcmpm0683993ssy86s77kavf`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nReducing the treasury tax from 20% to 10% threatens the long-term sustainability of the Cardano ecosystem. The current tax ensures stability by funding future growth and development. Cutting it primarily benefits large stake pools while limiting the treasury’s ability to support innovation and resilience in an uncertain future.\r\nThe treasury is a key mechanism for decentralization and sustainability, fueled by transaction-based contributions that reflect active ecosystem engagement. Weakening its replenishment risks concentrating governance power in a few wallets, reducing meaningful circulation and long-term viability.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "LallyMcClay",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmWHnY8vUfGp1V2pdEVYxsPzgjxwdhbVxi5EJ4nPAJkmh2 -->\r\n# LallyMcClay\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2dptycrks2ggae95leeh66m0l0svet98c44hl4rmaec7as6hgpp3`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nRationale for  \"No\" vote for the GA proposal to reduce the treasury cut from 20% to 10%. \r\n\r\nTLDR: While you could argue this proposal may offer short-term benefits for stakers, on balance I feel the risk of undermining long-term sustainability and resilience of Cardano’s ecosystem is greater. I also believe economic parameters shouldn't be considered or changed in isolation. My line of reasoning supports a cautious approach that prioritises the long term stability and strategic capacity of the treasury over a possible modest increases in staking rewards.\r\n\r\n\r\nLong-Term Stability Over Short-Term Gains:\r\nYes -  boosting staking rewards is an attractive prospect,  but the treasury's robust funding is crucial to maintain long-term ecosystem health and resilience especially if we have market downturns, unforeseen challenges or \"Black Swan\" events .\r\nI do not believe  that there would be a significant staking reward increase, and the relatively small increase in rewards doesn't, in my view,  justify the risk of depleting funds needed for future developments, and could leave the network vulnerable to unforeseen events.\r\n\r\nSpeculative Compensation Assumption:\r\nI feel this proposal is made with an assumption or expectation that Ada’s price will consistently appreciate to make up for the reduced treasury inflows, which cannot be certain. \r\n\r\nMaintaining a Strategic Reserve:\r\nThe treasury also acts as a financial safety net. This supports Cardano's long-term innovation and security and is referred to in the Constitution.  Reducing its funding could limit the network's ability in responding appropriately to needs and opportunities.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "LEADStakePool",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lead-pool/DRep/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context.jsonld -->\r\n# LEADStakePool\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Decrease Treasury Tax from 20% to 10%\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytnvazqpsj98pu7w6vtmeddcy9pnn6y6nl0psr5ajzge52s82zz8t`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWe discussed this internally with delegators who are part of our telegram channel... Our rational for voting yes is as follows:\r\na) Increases rewards for delegators\r\nb) We want to see parameters tweaked and do not believe we should be afraid to try things - especially given what we have seen with other parameters such as k and a0\r\nc) We can always change back or adjust the parameter again if needed\r\nd) Treasury will still be sizable\r\ne) We do not know fully yet how funds will be spent and it could be interesting to see how they go with the first round",
      "vote": "YES"
