    {
      "voter": "Cardano Cypherpunks",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreidql3v4lizmpyszirotlyj556gkt5anvrpozjnrceavybllbdftoe -->\r\n# Cardano Cypherpunks\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** CARDANO BLOCKCHAIN ECOSYSTEM CONSTITUTION v2.0\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y23wk4w6f2aumc92vl9m3x74xzm6jh4ha9wdq09yfq2sl6q8zlv4l`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThese changes largely reinforce cypherpunk principles by embedding censorship resistance, user sovereignty, and transparency into the governance framework while codifying checks on power via on-chain rules and liquid democracy. While some changes introduce additional formalism (audits, codes of conduct, committee powers), they do not fundamentally undermine cypherpunk values so long as participation remains voluntary, rights of exit are preserved, and the system remains anchored in code and cryptography rather than politics. On balance, v2.0 is an evolutionary improvement toward a more resilient and decentralized governance model, even if it nudges the culture slightly closer to institutional accountability than radical minimalism.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cardano Foundation",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmSFzHp48K3zUzP7itVBbCKcVdBfaB89npp1H8ykznJatn -->\r\n# Cardano Foundation\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** CARDANO BLOCKCHAIN ECOSYSTEM CONSTITUTION v2.0\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ydpfkyjxzeqvalf6fgvj7lznrk8kcmfnvy9hyl6gr6ez6wgsjaelx`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/Qma7zK4H6CuvqBqDkmkErtwegHcoAuvzTaQAEnUjGmhDVa\r\n\r\nThis governance action has generated significant community discussion, much of which is focused on the drafting process, as it did not include global workshops and voting at a convention. We believe that governance documents for a decentralized infrastructure like Cardano should be improved incrementally and that any community member should be allowed to make suggestions. While in-person processes have their place and can be useful, we do not agree that this should be a requirement for any proposed changes.\r\n\r\nConsequently, we focused on evaluating the substance of a proposal and are voting on whether it represents a net positive for the Cardano ecosystem. In our assessment this proposal followed the amendment process as it is currently required by the Constitution. While there were no workshops or conventions, the drafting process involved months of public, asynchronous feedback via shared documents where anyone could contribute. We welcome further refinement of the constitutional amendment process and we see the Civics Committee as a potential body to lead this work. The Cardano Foundation is eager to collaborate with them to explore a structured process for future amendments. However, at this stage we do not wish to vote against an amendment which we believe is a net positive for the system simply because it did not follow the previous drafting process.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Assessment of the Proposed Version\r\nThe  new version makes several key enhancements that we believe will make Cardano's governance more effective:\r\n1. **A More Efficient Treasury Process:** The proposal streamlines the treasury process by removing the separate \"budget info action\" and integrating its requirements directly into a single, more comprehensive treasury withdrawal governance action type. This reduces bureaucracy while maintaining scrutiny, as the new draft introduces stricter informational requirements for all treasury withdrawals. Some of these requirements will provide DReps with greater certainty as to who is receiving the withdrawal and what they intend to use it for, thereby providing better transparency for the ecosystem on the use of treasury funds.\r\n\r\n2. **Enhanced Clarity and Governance Hygiene:** The draft introduces clearer definitions, fixes many inconsistencies, and removes outdated or unused clauses. These changes make the Constitution a more precise and self-contained document while reducing ambiguity for all governance participants. \r\n\r\n3. **Upholding Core Governance Principles:** This update refines the Constitution's mechanics without altering the fundamental balance of power or the core roles of DReps, SPOs, and the Constitutional Committee.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "CardanoYoda (MANDA Pool)",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmdncQLeyhPeF8U71L4F6LmE8S2Fu7suyyqXB1FwrssGv7 -->\r\n# CardanoYoda (MANDA Pool)\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** CARDANO BLOCKCHAIN ECOSYSTEM CONSTITUTION v2.0\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2m0g4r66pyaw3p7u454wc0p4f0ygm8ueaev0mgd3tvwm7sskqwqp`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmXtbyaP2rzMW4kBABckREZAF6P4HKR5xP6KbvW5KUPD2T\r\n\r\nWhile many changes have been made, most are structural—streamlined wording, improved clarity, fixed typos, and the removal of redundancies.\r\n\r\nThe most significant update is the elimination of the requirement for a Budget Information Governance Action before a Withdrawal Governance Action. Removing this prerequisite will cut unnecessary bureaucracy and improve the efficiency of funding.\r\n\t\r\nA few additional updates have also been introduced. Notably, for treasury withdrawals totaling under 1 million ADA within a two-year period, several previously burdensome requirements have been lifted. This adjustment streamlines the process for small-scale funding and reduces unnecessary constraints.\r\n\r\nI want to express my sincere gratitude to everyone who contributed to the amendment of the Constitution.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cerkoryn",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmVZekb2Cf4CuWr5tLyjhSkhTh1rDf8687DiLvgMgm2XYM -->\r\n# Cerkoryn\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** CARDANO BLOCKCHAIN ECOSYSTEM CONSTITUTION v2.0\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yglrf4el8gghum239fggvfrau25k2576y4dvcz65r2ukj8sqpsc2k`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAfter reviewing the proposed changes to the Cardano Constitution, I have decided to lean toward a YES vote on this proposal.  I think that the changes are an improvement over the existing Constitution, but there is still a lot more to be desired.\r\n\r\nThe Good:\r\n-Removal of the Budget Info Action and establishing the auditing/administrator/oversight requirements in the Treasury Withdrawal instead.\r\n-Removing requirement for dRep Codes of Conduct (as these were generally unenforceable anyways).\r\n-Adding a requirement for immutable URLs to governance actions.\r\n\r\nThe Bad:\r\n-Clarity on the NCL is still somewhat lacking.\r\n-Staggering CC members. I'm not sure how this would work given that the ledger only recognizes a single CC at a time.  Additionally, I don't think it is enforceable.\r\n-Treasury withdrawals requiring the identity of the recipient. To me this seems like it would implement a hard KYC requirement and not allow pseudonymous community figures (i.e., Hosky, Cardano Whale, etc.) from ever proposing a treasury withdrawal.\r\n-\"DReps have voting power equal to the number of Lovelace delegated to them.\" This would prevent changes such as quadratic voting power, which I think is something that should be left open for exploration.\r\n-Required disclosures for people who hold multiple roles (dRep, SPO, CC). Also required disclosures for compensation.  I don't think these are enforceable. And if they were, it could be the case that a single dRep refusing to disclose something might make the entire governance action unconstitutional.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Chris Cata",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmPyhbZNbH3qvULnom7xafNVfbt2bg17Jz46DWDVP9YTab -->\r\n# Chris Cata\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** CARDANO BLOCKCHAIN ECOSYSTEM CONSTITUTION v2.0\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfdfs28uwafjgmrkatdektlzrvha2cmvqjhuz700e04mawq23rmrg`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWhile I am for continuous improvement, I have a couple of key issues here where this goes a bit beyond what I think of as iterative. While Yuta has gone through some lengths to collect feedback from the community this is absent, in my opinion, of broader community involved problem solving methods outside certain areas of social media feedback.  It attempts to make a significant change to the budget process with insufficient explanation, reasoning, or guidance – as well as removes CC “gates” put in place when it comes to the original BIA – and perhaps its intent. **The result here is a NO vote from me.**\r\nWhere would I vote yes? I want to see a narrowed approach to the submitted changes (more than one proposal effectively) – with comprehensive and focused changes to key areas with expanded upon reasonings - which I would prefer to see for a change to the constitution at this level.  The brief set of information in the proposal in my opinion lacks sufficient supporting links/evidence of the pros and cons of those larger non-insignificant changes (NCL, BIA, CC scope/impact). Even the link to the Google doc used to collect initial information from community members is missing here, which could have been a supporting link (although still not sufficient for me). Below is perhaps an iterative roadmap on constitutional changes that will be easier to move forward with.\r\n\r\n**Proposal 1 – Constitution Update v1.1 ** - What I see as fixes to typos and additional definitions, clearer or narrower scopes defined amongst the roles, and ultimately some tidiness around guardrails and their groupings (network, economic, governance, etc.) – this should have been 1 proposal in and of itself. Easy win.\r\n**Proposal 2 – Constitution Update v1.5 Suggestion** - I would have considered a second constitutional update proposal when it came to the NCL.  Shoring up the definition and aligning the budget process to fit around those needs. There is still debates ongoing about how to handle this, so we should not look beyond this because of the downstream impacts this definition may have in the process. \r\n**Proposal – Constitution Update v2.0 Suggestion**  - Finally – if v1.5 did not resolve key process issues around the BIA and TWA - create another more narrowed/focused constitutional update to address budget/treasury related process changes specifically as it will make fundamental changes to the budget process, change the CC scope/”gated” process how it is today, and more things I’m forgetting to mention here.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
