    {
      "voter": "BEACNpool",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreifmvtlxhaerzh4cuphzlmpd4x5lgc2bqe3czt3a4vm6jyv67lsulq -->\r\n# BEACNpool\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Net Change Limit of 300 Million ADA for Epochs 613–713\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2jn8fk0cn6wd6et3evnykea0glhw7t20xnhwss4xxjlczq29343n`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI’m voting YES because establishing a clear Net Change Limit (NCL) for a defined period is a constitutional / process requirement and this proposal is reasonable, data-anchored, and time-aligned with treasury planning.\r\n\r\nWhy YES\r\n\r\nMeets the governance need: An NCL must be set for a specific period so the ecosystem has an explicit, auditable constraint on net treasury change. Approving an NCL avoids ambiguity and ad‑hoc decision-making.\r\nPrudent fiscal discipline: 300,000,000 ADA is set by reference to the prior year’s actual treasury inflows (Epochs 532–604) totaling 306,940,195 ADA. Setting the NCL slightly below that figure is conservative and supports sustainability.\r\nGood timing window (613–713): The proposed range aligns the NCL cycle with the mid‑year budget season, and ensures a complete prior year of inflow data is available when the next NCL is set—improving predictability and legitimacy of future budgeting.\r\nPredictability for builders + voters: A clearly defined cap for a long window improves planning for treasury-funded work while preserving an explicit limit that voters can enforce.\r\nNotes / expectations\r\n\r\nThis is an Info Action establishing the NCL; it doesn’t approve any specific spending by itself.\r\nFuture treasury withdrawals should continue to be evaluated on deliverables, milestones, and public receipts, within the approved NCL.\r\n(Reference: Governance Action ID gov_action1…tft9tp; metadata anchor ipfs://bafkreid7tr5uotr4bp2jp54q7lbm5p5eldncuahprj53msy6kqgjscyrdu, hash 4e264b46…c0588b.)",
