    {
      "voter": "BWtake(長崎)Japan",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreiclnjjgm3xbhs2u2hpxed7nkqgndfqyu3dyif5x4b4fwvla35ywre -->\r\n# BWtake(長崎)Japan\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytcw6qzpqqclx2yd0zy64ztvlkkhnf6yrzza8whgnq4vz5gh89626`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n隗より始めよ。\r\n改革はまず、自らが模範を示すところから始まる――この提案はまさにその実践です。\r\n憲法委員会は、Cardanoの最高意思決定の合憲性を担保し、\r\nトレジャリーの運用や憲法改正など、数百億円規模の公共資産の正当性を審査する機関です。\r\nこれは企業にたとえれば、監査委員会と取締役会の中枢を兼ねるレベルの責任に相当します。\r\n\r\n年間₳200,000（約2,000万円）は、一見大きく見えても、\r\n同等の重責を担う企業役員やガバナンス専門職の報酬と比べれば決して過大ではなく、むしろ控えめです。\r\nCardanoが本気で「公共インフラとしてのブロックチェーン」を築くなら、\r\nその中核を担う人材には相応の敬意と報酬が必要です。\r\n\r\n憲法委員会が自ら透明な設計を提示したことを高く評価し、\r\n公正で持続可能なガバナンスの礎として、この提案に賛成します。\r\n\r\n—\r\n\r\nLead by example.\r\nTrue reform begins when those in charge set the standard themselves — and this proposal does exactly that.\r\nThe Constitutional Committee holds immense responsibility, ensuring the legality of governance actions and safeguarding billions in public treasury funds.\r\nIn corporate terms, this role is comparable to a board director and audit chair combined.\r\n\r\nAn annual cap of 200,000 ADA (around $130,000–$150,000) is fair and even modest compared to executives in major institutions with similar levels of accountability.\r\nTransparent and reasonable compensation is essential to attract capable, independent members and to sustain Cardano’s governance over time.\r\n\r\nBy defining its own compensation publicly and on-chain, the Committee sets a precedent for fairness and integrity.\r\nI support this proposal as a practical and symbolic step toward a mature, sustainable Cardano governance system.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "CardaFlow",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmXPPW2AVvfBrMT1bQDmo2Pd4QKp9ZAtNaToMdeToKhHFV -->\r\n# CardaFlow\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y279cte2lujj9lde4lmz07wsku5fvra69htrleyx9ahr60cn42uaa`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nPlease get me right, I think that CC members should be paid for their mandate.\r\n\r\nHowever, we are a blockchain ecosystem at the eve of AI revolution, and we would compensate 100,000 USD/y/members for assessing few hundreds gov actions constitutionality ?\r\n\r\nThis is what we would have paid in 2000s, not what we should pay in 2025 in my opinion... I would not spend more than the quarter of it and spend the difference to build an AI-driven CC assessment tool for members...",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cardanians",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmNhfRbts8hkzr4iLKgBs5WszeodBTmLjZ7b8DXDD1u3u7 -->\r\n# Cardanians\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yt4lyf0fwlrz8k6j5evd3rxw0sqp67qkh36su98a97q3qsc0h03y4`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWe voted No because the requested 1,000,000 ADA compensation for the Constitutional Committee is not proportionate to the committee’s limited mandate and lacks sufficient justification or workload transparency. The proposal sets an unsustainable precedent for administrative spending, prioritizing institutional salaries over broader ecosystem needs. While smart-contract oversight and reporting are positive, the overall scale and structure do not meet our standards for efficient, accountable treasury use.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cardano Cypherpunks",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreig6ze7i7yhyzy7mgwdxrqqjkzdk5qdltbjo3q2pgyufwfrrrtzizu -->\r\n# Cardano Cypherpunks\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Abstain\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y23wk4w6f2aumc92vl9m3x74xzm6jh4ha9wdq09yfq2sl6q8zlv4l`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm open to compensating the CC, but would prefer a smaller amount for the first year",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cardano Foundation",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmcEsY9XrrCdU1w5nkQdUrhkfEtAux99wuEPQa1phFrZby -->\r\n# Cardano Foundation\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ydpfkyjxzeqvalf6fgvj7lznrk8kcmfnvy9hyl6gr6ez6wgsjaelx`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmYCnQDYKk1RMjxGV5XoxkG41wv8f8T9JK7GyPXd6iXqUs\r\n\r\nThis governance action has successfully initiated an important, ecosystem-wide discussion on how to fairly and sustainably compensate key governance roles. The Cardano Constitution explicitly provides for the compensation of the Constitutional Committee (CC). The Cardano Foundation, acting as a DRep, supports this principle.\r\n\r\nOur **NO** vote is not a rejection of the principle of compensation but of this specific implementation. Our concerns are based on the following points:\r\n- **Improper Sequencing:** This proposal is premature as it requests funding before a necessary accountability framework is in place. Compensating these key role players without a framework against which to hold them accountable for performing their duties is unwise. A public-facing framework defining the CC's duties, minimum participation standards, and conditions for non-payment should be established before a budget for compensation is approved.\r\n- **Misaligned Incentive Structure:** A high, pre-determined compensation amount of 200,000 ada per seat without an accountability framework risks creating a problematic incentive structure. It may attract future candidates who are i) unsuited to the role (by background, incentive or expertise) and ii) primarily motivated by the payment rather than by commitment to fulfilling their role  - potentially compromising the quality of the CC in subsequent terms.\r\n- **Potential Governance Design Issue:** The proposal's justification of a high workload points to a potential governance design flaw. If the burden on a small number of CC members is unsustainable, the solution should be to fix the governance design (e.g., by expanding the CC to distribute the load, and incentivising CC members to take decisions efficiently) rather than compensating a few members to endure a structural bottleneck.\r\n- **Lack of a Calculation for the Proposed Amount:** The proposal does not explain how the 200,000 ada figure per seat was calculated. This makes it hard to know whether this figure is based on estimated time and effort, and if it considers single-person CC members vs collectives. The approximate effort in reviewing GAs per seat should be equal and not differ drastically. \r\n\r\n## Recommendations for a Future Submission\r\nThis \"no\" vote is intended to be constructive. We believe a supportable CC budget is achievable and would be prepared to review a future submission that addresses the following key points:\r\n- **Establish an Accountability Framework First:** A future proposal should be preceded by, or at least contain, a clear public-facing framework, such as a formal Code of Conduct. This document should define the minimum duties, participation levels (e.g., voting frequency, rationale submission), and specific conditions under which a member would not be eligible for compensation.\r\n- **Propose a Modular and Flexible Payment Structure:** Instead of a single, automatic budget, a more defensible model would be modular (e.g., paid per governance action reviewed on a monthly basis) and tied to active participation. This ensures compensation is for services rendered and allows the system to flexibly manage costs if a member resigns or becomes inactive.\r\n- **Address the Governance Design:** We encourage a broader discussion on the underlying problem of workload. A long-term solution should focus on improving the governance design itself, which may in turn lead to a more sustainable and cost-effective compensation model.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "CardanoYoda (MANDA Pool)",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmQ6eQgZbBYe5T1NzvaNUpfsht5R5QWCpUTiSvfLw6q4Yg -->\r\n# CardanoYoda (MANDA Pool)\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2m0g4r66pyaw3p7u454wc0p4f0ygm8ueaev0mgd3tvwm7sskqwqp`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmWpAqASFtKoeWm3MuGZK4QriBVDLGbLSVLfqiSnZZhfZ8\r\n\r\nTo the best of my knowledge, the proposal was not preceded by any debate with DReps and the community. CC members only debated among themselves. Only 5 out of 7 CC members are requesting compensation. The details of this debate are not public.\r\n\r\nThe proposal seems to me inconceptual and does not address any specific problem that we can currently observe. If we are to make changes, we should stick to a vision and strategy. It makes no sense to introduce compensation for one group of governance bodies without knowing how we will compensate DReps. We also do not know what the community and DReps think about compensation.\r\n\r\nI will not approve compensation for CC members without simultaneously introducing the same benefits for DReps.\r\n\r\nI dare say that the work of DReps is much more demanding than the work of CC members.\r\n\r\n- While CC members are elected from a relatively small number of interested parties and then have their position secured, DReps have to try harder to get delegations from ADA holders. This is time-consuming.\r\n\r\n- CC members only deal with the constitutionality of proposals, which is just a binary decision based on a clear text of the Constitution. If the proposal is in accordance with the Constitution, CC members just vote YES (regardless of their opinion). DReps have to deal with the merit of proposals, which is more time-consuming. It is necessary to analyze all aspects of proposals from different perspectives. They have to communicate with teams and delegators. In some cases, they are even asked for their opinion before submitting proposals. They have to give detailed feedback to teams (if they want and have the time).\r\n\r\n- DReps have to explain their decisions to the community much more often, communicate with their delegates, and even face criticism. One prominent DRep even retired after such criticism. Also in this respect, the work of DReps is more demanding.\r\n\r\n- It is DReps who approve proposals. While CC members can all vote YES for a proposal that is in accordance with the Constitution, it is up to DReps to reject a proposal that has low impact, or is even submitted by fraudsters. The role of DReps is more responsible.\r\n\r\n- CC members have performed their role flawlessly without compensation so far. Many DReps are not willing to write rationales. Many DReps do not even explicitly vote NO if they do not want to vote YES. Perhaps their fatigue can be observed. In this case, compensation could address specific governance issues (there are plenty of issues).\r\n\r\nIf the position of CC members were to become more lucrative than the position of DReps due to compensation, it could introduce an imbalance into the system.\r\n\r\nIf there is agreement that CC members deserve compensation, I dare say that DReps deserve it too.\r\n\r\nWhile in the case of CC members, introducing compensation is relatively easy, since there is a fixed number of them and their voting power is the same. In the case of DReps, it would be more complicated. They have different voting power and a different number of delegators.\r\n\r\nThis complexity is not a reason to introduce compensation for CC members before we introduce it for DReps.\r\n\r\nIntersect requires a relatively large sum for Cardano governance. Governance tools also require funding. Let’s define the vision, determine how much we want to spend on governance from the Treasury, and then make decisions regarding other costs.\r\n\r\nVolunteer work can have its limits and shortcomings. If DReps, but also CC members, put more time and effort into their work, maybe it would be beneficial for Cardano. Compensation would be attractive to experts, which we definitely need.\r\n\r\nMy opinion is that we should not pay governance bodies through Treasury withdrawals. Instead, we should integrate governance with staking. But that would be a different debate.\r\n\r\nIf we were to use 1M ADA for compensation, let’s divide this amount between CC members and active DReps. Governance bodies decide on hundreds of millions of ADA per year. This is a responsible role that should probably be compensated. However, doing it voluntarily is still a valid option.\r\n\r\nMaybe it would be fair to do an analysis first and discuss the pros and cons. This proposal is unprepared and completely ignores the complexity of Cardano governance.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cash",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/Qmf3rutJ8Dhu191B5WmzqZBzmQp8FJF97PDGvN1o3sT8Vy -->\r\n# Cash\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygeyfh8nm03dnl5a2hxdtv09pu7uhep9l0cpg0zpr60jqys05cku2`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI agree that CC members should be compensated. That said, this proposal is lacking critical budgetary details that would allow me to make an informed decision.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "CASIA- Cardano Asia Team",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmPfRXchTaPYwWtDiSdSGc8HjmNMLazdcmWn7Ns74qCRcD -->\r\n# CASIA- Cardano Asia Team\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytcy6lfas0zu4axuzw2ncal2k2mh6u8srt5c63fpgq3zg8cd2437d`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nno, this vote is not transparent from the begining and only the CC voted yes for this, which is self-interest conflict\r\n\r\nIt should be together with CC term and transparent when voting at the beginning with clear kPI and responsibility",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Cerkoryn",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Cerkoryn/governance-reference/refs/heads/main/voting-history/info/Info_18.jsonld -->\r\n# Cerkoryn\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yglrf4el8gghum239fggvfrau25k2576y4dvcz65r2ukj8sqpsc2k`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am supportive of the idea of constitutional committee members receiving some compensation, but I feel like this ask is too much too soon.  It is my opinion that this compensation should be more of a supplementary income rather than a full-time salary.  Additionally, I feel like the amount asked for, per seat, is too much.  In the future I would consider supporting a smaller amount of compensation, and probably even a smaller constitutional committee.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Chris Cata",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreidg44ebhvjae72z7sgj4qdqqgkvbsjqr462h3x4mgx24ys6yoiomu -->\r\n# Chris Cata\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfdfs28uwafjgmrkatdektlzrvha2cmvqjhuz700e04mawq23rmrg`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am trying to quantify/qualify the 1M ADA (or 500ADA/day per CC) and I'm not there with this proposal, however I will be voting YES to signal being in favor of compensation CC members overall.  What I wish to see is some other mechanism/proof of work(?) tied to compensation to qualify for payment.  In such a way that CC members would be eligible 'up to' 200K ADA, but they do not all equally earn it. In my mind, just trying to balance CC members that put in X hours vs others that put in Y hours. I hate to ask of more from CC members, but I really do feel that it should be something in place.  The 1M ADA is arbitrary to me at this point, and I do wish to see it tied an actual USD amount - and basing payouts on a flat number so that ADA being withdrawn for payment can go up or down depending on the cost but we all agree what that's going to be in USD.  This presents challenges in what that ought to be but again, to me that's important.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Christopher Erckert Dotare",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmemmMg1TX6Sv8ZHwxeZVJdpmtQt9Aegcv5ZEmJAMAKgJQ -->\r\n# Christopher Erckert Dotare\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yg8aktnf09gdd0jpyhuz3fy22mfjrqrg2ru52907zeeeeqcra6xhe`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWe should compensate governance as long as we can govern compensation.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Crypto Eats Legacy",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmQ1ReRp1KBwZmrFeoQAifuwcMzE6eoBK49SgKGXWgLzcZ -->\r\n# Crypto Eats Legacy\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytd2mf58tfqv78z7e99fy0gnmfep0kscclnwpvx7qvwpc5srxwg5g`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThe proposed amount seems too high. A lower price would be reasonable.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Crypto_Oaj",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmRyNGXvq7X4CXnFAdVJXYm4zFTzLCHSeht1kRHdZYVXV4 -->\r\n# Crypto_Oaj\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytwlmrm6ksc8sa5fdhm63prqve6hjzvghdc3053zwz6hyeg0ayhg2`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am voting YES on this proposal, as I fully agree with the requested amount and the overall approach to providing fair remuneration to the Constitution Committee (CC) members. Recognizing and compensating their time, responsibility, and contribution to Cardano’s governance structure is crucial for maintaining accountability and sustaining long-term participation.\r\n\r\nHowever, I do have an open question that I’d like the community and my delegators to reflect on: the current structure excludes individual CC members—such as KtorZ and Phil_UPLC—from receiving payment simply because they serve independently rather than as part of a group. This could unintentionally discourage future candidates from running solo, incentivizing people to form artificial groups or organizational entities just to qualify for funding.\r\n\r\nWhile I understand that multi-member or script-based CCs may spend more time coordinating and reaching consensus, both individual and collective CCs fulfill the same constitutional responsibilities. The mechanism for balancing these payments between individuals and groups deserves deeper discussion within the community to ensure fairness and maintain motivation for all future CC candidates.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Dan",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmbT7UM1xmY1EGSQwCYCra8TNT9nARRGAaag6yYTaFz873 -->\r\n# Dan\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Abstain\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfsgx4dqfcxxs0ms533twzs7w0cvw8vq8qwzmjyrp280jfsl8gusv`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nVoting: Abstain\r\n\r\nI support compensating CC members. The role demands time, constitutional familiarity, and ongoing engagement, and compensation helps ensure participation isn’t limited to well-resourced groups or individuals. Funding the CC from a neutral source aligns with the constitution and supports institutional independence.\r\n\r\nHowever, the request doesn’t provide enough detail to assess whether 200,000 ada per member is proportionate to the work involved. It’s unclear how many individuals within each group will receive compensation, whether any group members are opting out, or how responsibilities are shared. Without this information, it’s difficult to judge whether the proposed amount fairly reflects the effort required.\r\n\r\nBased on publicly available information, the estimated number of individuals in each requesting group is:\r\n\r\n- Cardano Atlantic Council: 6\r\n- Tingvard: 6\r\n- Eastern Cardano Council: 7\r\n- Ace Alliance: 5\r\n- Cardano Japan Council: 5\r\n\r\nIf all individuals are requesting compensation, the per-person amount would fall roughly between 28,500 and 40,000 ada, but this is still an assumption. A clear breakdown of (1) who is being compensated, (2) how the internal allocation works, and (3) the expected time commitments per individual would allow DReps to assess the fairness of the request with more confidence.\r\n\r\nFuture requests would also benefit from a transparent compensation framework that accounts for both multi-individual groups and single-individual members. Even though no solo members are seeking compensation this round, establishing this now would be useful. A base amount per CC member plus an additional amount per individual contributor would better align compensation with workload and avoid disadvantaging single-member seats.\r\n\r\nGiven the lack of clarity and the difficulty of verifying whether the budget is appropriate, I am voting Abstain. The proposal is directionally sound, but it needs clearer justification and a transparent compensation structure before it can be properly evaluated.",
      "vote": "ABSTAIN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Dave",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmZVNGK3q3ifX32qpKxuiXqpxSQipVXVYNLBNGdSodUTkX -->\r\n# Dave\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygsgfhcydhlfglamhzkjn97rz3edef8a4z99fwl2frcwnrcmgurt3`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmZBbzRv1VYBNfNSR5XcYwGXvo3JNwBFshiz6AWLzna8UW\r\n\r\nI am voting **NO**. While I greatly value our CC members and the critical role they perform, this ask is way too high.\r\n\r\nMy concern is that it creates an unfair compensation gap between the CC and other vital contributors, additionally it sets a standard which is difficult to reverse. More importantly, this committee **stepped in and committed to this role without any promise of compensation** for this term.\r\n\r\nFor the sake of our long-term **sustainability** and **decentralization**, I believe we need more governance maturity before we can commit to a spend like this.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Dr. Navjit Dhaliwal",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmTSFuRCBVdTnr9jGYeyTt2CgjdjJCTLNj6fAPN1x19eCE -->\r\n# Dr. Navjit Dhaliwal\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfya5pwcm2zw0e60c3pkqscq6spge7m0k6jtxkfsymv68gs2z56e5`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI think it's important to compensate people for their work.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1y2jyvvrk9g8t7uhmdtxp9nxay7a9rvj5ktqyd59wfe7ncmqege0em",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreihe7v2bfmv2q3xfba7fu7g37yfu345aocfej5p3sxkneivjqppoii -->\r\n# drep1y2jyvvrk9g8t7uhmdtxp9nxay7a9rvj5ktqyd59wfe7ncmqege0em\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2jyvvrk9g8t7uhmdtxp9nxay7a9rvj5ktqyd59wfe7ncmqege0em`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nthat amount of compensation seems excessive and no explanation on what exactly the compensation is for. treasury funds should be used for eco system growth and responsible spending.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1y2s6uvp5lq9dlp6vxydhmcpq4yr68v9sxkyu8agyzmw0kps2t8ugh",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmRbbmW9wqUnYKSnkXhaCFoTYDX9o4cersEHzjUny9nUcf -->\r\n# drep1y2s6uvp5lq9dlp6vxydhmcpq4yr68v9sxkyu8agyzmw0kps2t8ugh\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2s6uvp5lq9dlp6vxydhmcpq4yr68v9sxkyu8agyzmw0kps2t8ugh`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nPersonally I don’t see a problem with paying people for time spent on governance and especially not constitutionality. \r\n\r\nNo one had a problem with using funds to hold events to educate the community through workshops and involve them in the process. \r\n\r\nI don’t see the issue with paying to uphold and maintain what we spent money on creating.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1yf6xkrz2w4fdkwjw9u98u3pmzk7c7a0amq95hakcj649dns2r4upl",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmbYobvTP8TxdL4Sz6ovKLwNEGTTb1u4zTgjVianwG879z -->\r\n# drep1yf6xkrz2w4fdkwjw9u98u3pmzk7c7a0amq95hakcj649dns2r4upl\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yf6xkrz2w4fdkwjw9u98u3pmzk7c7a0amq95hakcj649dns2r4upl`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmUs59Fxfgk1RduoFAyFzcCLpMu6jNX9sxUmdN3qMPQMsn\r\n\r\nIm not categorically against compensating CC members, but the amount is so outrageously high that it warps incentives for future CC candidates.\r\nHonestly? Id even consider 10k per CC seat (yes. thats 70k total, not per individual CC member) a lot.\r\n\r\nCompensation also massively disincentivises CC consortiums as it would benefit individuals the most. In which case, we would literally need to rely on the individual opinions and moodswings of just 7 people.\r\n\r\nI think a reasonable amount would be between 1000 and 5000 ada per year per seat. Thats low enough to not be a reason to apply and high enough to cover material costs, and/or a group dinner - considerable as \"thanks\" from the community.\r\n\r\n## Looked at from a different angle:\r\nThe whole blockchain currently earns about 3.6m ada every year in fees.\r\nIf 1m or 27% of the total revenue of the chain would be the cost to securing constitutionality in a programmable environment, then the trifecta governance model should be deemed at a failure.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1yfcxs7sxzjd2ls7gjjftqmn58gg2q5fjwdcaznu0jh3uvpgceznnu",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreignci74pt3s27huhhg33zh76mxcq3am4ls6mi7e7tx5eodzel4qgq -->\r\n# drep1yfcxs7sxzjd2ls7gjjftqmn58gg2q5fjwdcaznu0jh3uvpgceznnu\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfcxs7sxzjd2ls7gjjftqmn58gg2q5fjwdcaznu0jh3uvpgceznnu`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nsupport fair CC compensation to ensure unbiased and professional governance.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1ygn28gh7gpr53re3e4nnvqqz4kv2pve0nty44qrfgqfk74gxqavlg",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AdaLovelace777/CC-Payments-/refs/heads/main/Vote_Context%20(56).jsonld -->\r\n# drep1ygn28gh7gpr53re3e4nnvqqz4kv2pve0nty44qrfgqfk74gxqavlg\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygn28gh7gpr53re3e4nnvqqz4kv2pve0nty44qrfgqfk74gxqavlg`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThe compensation is crucial because, unlike other roles within Cardano, CC members lack a neutral funding source to cover their significant administrative duties, which include reviewing governance actions, conducting research, and attending meetings. Providing this funding ensures the CC remains independent, accessible to a diverse range of qualified contributors, and prevents potential biases that could arise if members had to self-subsidize their participation or rely on external institutions. Still, I think the number here is too large, and I don't want this to turn into the United States where public service is a for profit job that only the most corrupt take in order to make large sums of money by whatever means necessary. These CC members should want to work for free as Ada succeeding already pays them. Their payment is when we 10x. This way, they are greatly rewarded for investing with US!",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1ytulwjetfmltznfa3d4rz545fdcl884kwp6zfzchazqpvtsv4ckzq",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreibivyxmmk5gcpy5v54eivbqmk2txz74ggtvp44gwkatce3p3rm75u -->\r\n# drep1ytulwjetfmltznfa3d4rz545fdcl884kwp6zfzchazqpvtsv4ckzq\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytulwjetfmltznfa3d4rz545fdcl884kwp6zfzchazqpvtsv4ckzq`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAs long as it follows constitutionality",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "drep1ytyemm3feqw3y7tsct90xt835tre4aewhe97dlcwx5l9lccmjmd4h",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreigsxuqkrgwuciw3l7mip5xxfxblwg3dgrrarz2wswj4irdafiufnq -->\r\n# drep1ytyemm3feqw3y7tsct90xt835tre4aewhe97dlcwx5l9lccmjmd4h\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytyemm3feqw3y7tsct90xt835tre4aewhe97dlcwx5l9lccmjmd4h`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n200,000 Ada is a bit much per person.  I would be much mor confortable with an amount about half of this value.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "EMURGO",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/EMGDRep/rationale/refs/heads/main/Vote_CC_Compensation.jsonld -->\r\n# EMURGO\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Abstain\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytvlwvyjmzfyn56n0zz4f6lj94wxhmsl5zky6knnzrf4jygpyahug`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nRationale\r\n\r\nWe are highly supportive of ensuring that Constitutional Committee members receive sustainable compensation for their meaningful contributions. Their constitutional oversight is central to Cardano’s governance, and the Constitution itself anticipates that key governance roles should be fairly and appropriately compensated.\r\n\r\nAt the Cardano Summit in Berlin, EMURGO and Intersect organized a brief session with DReps, CC members, stake pool operators, and other governance enthusiasts to gather broader feedback. It became apparent that there is a gap in how the proposer and the DReps interpret the clarity of this Budget Info action. \r\n\r\nSome DReps noted that the 1,000,000 ADA figure remains unclear, or that they are unsure where to find the most up-to-date supporting materials, while the proposer has provided certain rationales on X and other platforms.\r\n\r\nThis difference in perception highlights the need for a shared space where all relevant information can be presented transparently and discussed before moving forward. We acknowledge the materials and explanations provided by current CC members, but a common forum would help refine assumptions and strengthen collective understanding of the proposed compensation model.\r\n\r\nConclusion\r\n\r\nOur abstention reflects the need for additional clarity and alignment. We remain highly supportive of establishing sustainable compensation for the Constitutional Committee; however, this proposal requires clearer justification and stronger consensus across stakeholders before we can fully endorse it.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Eternl DRep Committee",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Tastenkunst/eternl-drep/refs/heads/main/gov_action_00056_vote_context.jsonld -->\r\n# Eternl DRep Committee\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y29h2q6cst2pvkl2sqqvf5ljcy36uv7pmy482xnczddzgqshus24w`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nTL;DR: EDC voted NO on gov_action16f69yf2f35w9ds8sr05ewyr55jg5f439mu8882rv295fvf8m4kcqql7y6nc;\r\n\r\nThe proposal lacks clarity. How many people are in each CC group? How much funding does each individual request? We see that the two individual CC participants do not request any funding, and the same might apply to some individuals within the CC groups. 200,000 ADA is a large sum for one year, roughly equivalent to a full-time salary for three people, depending on the country they live in. In any case, we would like to see more details about the discussions that took place among the CC groups.\r\n\r\n(By the way, this info action is useful for starting discussions about the topic. Once the new Constitution is active, this will become a withdrawal action, which puts more pressure on the vote.)",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "GAIA",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmcKzmhdoLFS7SWFcgQHaRwVJjXgPYwdaPUvLNTspMT6Tb -->\r\n# GAIA\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytxr0lhg5j72wysva9ac2gsn4lck4gyen8ywxkqfchsqg3qs8uy44`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWhile I believe the Constitutional Committee should receive compensation out of all the Cardano representatives, the ADA ask is too much, so I must vote no.\r\n\r\nThe CC should have socialized this proposal before putting it on chain if they wanted to get it passed on the first go. \r\n\r\nWe as a community need to have the discussion of compensation for CC members and DReps, so perhaps this is how we now start.\r\n\r\nFeedback:\r\n\r\n- There needs to be rationale and calculations provided about how the amount requested of 200,000 ADA per CC seat was reached.\r\n\r\n- As far as I can tell, there are 23 individuals seeking compensation (hard to find out if there is one or more member of Tingvard). We can either assign payment based on seat or on individual participants. \r\n\r\nIn my opinion, I would expect no more than 2,000 ADA per month per person of compensation for the CC committee, so that works out to 24,000 ADA per person per year, and a total of 552,000 ADA (about half of what is being requested).\r\n\r\nThe main benefits of having a council of members per CC seat as opposed to a single member should be: efficiency in reaching a decision faster, splitting the workload, enabling a more democratic process.\r\n\r\nHaving a council of members per CC seat should not increase costs to ADA holders dramatically as opposed to having a single person hold a seat, and compensation needs to reflect this.\r\n\r\nWe also need to set an example to the community that getting a CC seat does not mean we are granting someone a golden goose. To prevent this from happening and to reinforce that splitting a CC seat should result in efficiency, not an overall increase in cost, I recommend that each individual CC seat be compensated no more than 50,000 ADA per year. This would set us at a reasonable 350,000 ADA for all 7 seats and incur a fair annual cost to the treasury and ADA holders.",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Gnobudy Special",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreigwibpuie7hmkgc4wynkcsgr4hfbd7v5sjitq73z5fjwi5e7layci -->\r\n# Gnobudy Special\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygd5gumlv8jnramte9z2zvksvncgt06hyds4k6naq52hq7c05svs5`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm in favour of professional compensation for a professional job. \r\nI have questions around the 'Dispute resolution' mechanism, whereby CC members may vote together to deny payment to 1 CC member. It seems fine while we're all friends and rowing in the same direction. But perhaps there should be another check/balance to prevent abuse of this particular power?",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Hermes Stakepool Germany (HRMS)",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreifj6uvmcmjk5r56xe2w2lo7v3vdjneimqwr3mep22emexec7anobm -->\r\n# Hermes Stakepool Germany (HRMS)\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygs5hngva3hymv7j4v0aewxpsjqskj9dnmy7j3jz5ven5zqvulq2t`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI think rewarding CC members is necessary in the long run. But 200k per member for one year is excessive even at current ADA price levels.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "hix_coffeepool",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmUux8Ngzb3xviqeZFtZdZzLP1U7wBJYhDV446M2X89Sm4 -->\r\n# hix_coffeepool\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygnh2uf4wkc8ldgfxwz7rzuga3m8jtqew9xh5g3n587mg6g3ge0sj`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am voting \"Yes\" for CC compansation. CC role is an essential feature of governance security, and their activities should be sufficiently supported by our ecosystem.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Homer J",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmQeCkXLPBEDiVHrageyX9SDna19NrseDffTKG94ADoHdx -->\r\n# Homer J\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2rvnp40ea4zsl4hqm0ev4qju205jx7mz57u2vwjzzrf50c24jca9`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nhttps://gifdb.com/gif/thank-you-for-volunteer-4szdrlioafgifad4.html",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "HOSKY",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://QmSQsA6on7miPgt1DBmaWkdRP1PhBmjAH2YDjc2868Pmyp -->\r\n# HOSKY\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Abstain\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yf2jzhuc4f7eu2yay9d9ta3dykxxcwn34wz8kak7nhd7vcgrxn7ns`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA [PDF version][pdf-link] of this rationale is also made available.\r\n\r\n[pdf-link]: https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmRYUVmC5iGcq7QXk3CEZxj9yj2DHUMQmi8uSiYdMmJ451\r\n\r\nI am abstaining from this vote as it involves compensation for Constitutional Committee members, which would be a conflict of interest given my current position. I still support the intent behind this proposal since fair compensation ensures qualified and unbiased individuals can dedicate the necessary time and attention to their duties.\r\n\r\nPaying members for their service encourages fairness, accountability, and professionalism. It allows capable contributors to focus fully on governance responsibilities and strengthens the ecosystem’s integrity.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "InputEndorsers",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreigcaehqzcki2svbf3qis2cfmicuf4ek6kjxwr2v4xeq4ncfspmxje -->\r\n# InputEndorsers\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y2hlgh9600zjlt39dh54z7z8d65kahneck8yhh7ugmzc9as0xrzqd`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nClock ticking. Not waiting 5-6 epochs to cast my vote. \r\nPreface to rationale: DRep compensation is a different ball game. DRep compensation would likely introduce a layer of new problems in minimum viable governance at this stage - through a proliferation of DRep-to earn players. \r\nNot so for the Constitutional Committee (CC). The CC should serve an independent and formidable barrier to governance capture. Especially when it comes to pressure from the big Cardano players, current or future. \r\nI set a personal DRep limit to approve up to ₳200-250M of spending in 1st year of minimum viable governance. I committed to vote YES to open source @cardano_govtool as my last YES vote this year (apart from emergencies). \r\nGovernance integrity in a period of minimum viable governance is a barrier to capture. The CC should be able to vote on the constitutionality of all motions - while dedicating sufficent time and effort to the process. Empowering a community elected, independent CC to protect Cardano from governance capture falls under emergency motions in my view, as the governance system is untested. \r\nConflicts of interest: I am not affiliated with the future CC members, apart from a staking relationship as an ADA holder. My SPO (TECH) is member of the Tingvard CC member. I have never met any of the CC members. TECH pool refunded part of the pool revenues to stakers in earlier epochs - and I benefited from that as one of the pool's many stakers.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Jakub Szyca",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jakubszyca/Cardano-Drep/refs/heads/main/Raw-Votes-Context/Vote_Context_6.jsonld -->\r\n# Jakub Szyca\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytee8ue66qdqkfquq5c9hld7trhxvqdra5plfhyuragcfsgrqen4n`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWhile I am not opposed to compensating different parts of Cardano governance in principle, I cannot support this governance action. First, the proposal provides insufficient justification for requesting 1,000,000 ADA. The amount appears arbitrary and, in my view, excessive. Second, compensation should apply equitably to all members of the Constitutional Committee, not only a selected few. This matter should be revisited only after the proponents provide a detailed rationale for determining appropriate compensation.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "jonahkoch",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreifsul7hnn55cb6udazexkxtybbyrzpmseytyljinpnapsuxpitjde -->\r\n# jonahkoch\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yt8trnz8e5cnylmyygt0pyzrfw92rt9s42kg8t4rhm6t0dstyvmdn`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm voting yes because the Constitutional Committee is the institutional backbone preventing governance collapse and treasury capture. Without a functioning CC, there's no constitutional enforcement, no treasury execution, and no governance. The system is paralyzed or devolves into a SPO directed hard fork. Expecting this work to continue unpaid isn't principled frugality, it's negligence that guarantees a select few, only the wealthy, self-serving, or corrupt serve.\r\nCC members carry real workload and legal liability. We should expect professionalism and accountability that protects the entire ecosystem from malicious actors and governance capture. The alternative to compensation is to compromise independence, invite corruption and risk stagnation.\r\nThis budget requests 1,000,000 ADA to compensate five of seven serving CC members for epochs 581-653. That's 200,000 ADA per seat, covering roughly 25 people total across multi-member councils. At current prices, this works out to under $20,000 USD per person annually far below market rate for equivalent legal and governance oversight in any comparable ecosystem. Two members (KtorZ and Phil_uplc) are declining compensation entirely, demonstrating this isn't a cash grab.\r\nThe Cardano Constitution explicitly mandates this in Article VII, Section 8: \"Cardano Blockchain ecosystem budgets shall provide for periodic administrative costs of the Constitutional Committee.\" This isn't discretionary, it's constitutional obligation. Administration runs through Intersect's audited smart contract framework with transparent milestone tracking and community oversight. Refusing to fund the CC while demanding they protect treasury integrity and constitutional compliance is hypocrisy. You can't expect professional performance from volunteer labor under legal liability.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Kevin G Mohr",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreibxxdlw2vcplcafblkspmu5nlmbjhh6773qvdl47npzeuszljhv2i -->\r\n# Kevin G Mohr\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytwn2u7qpy4ur7k3xqd7eehft3g2yjpxf46rugwzmndkyesd0m5nt`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nConstitution only guarantees administratice costs to be covered. The volume of work is too little for financial compensation. Furthermore, at this time, the CC should be a volunteer position.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Kyle Solomon",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/adosiawolf/drep-rationale/refs/heads/main/0053 -->\r\n# Kyle Solomon\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytzshxuma6cwrnlv2ucyclfqw3k4nu4nuudmh2z87j9hncsk9dhy4`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am voting YES because ensuring execution of Constitutional Committee duties is a responsibility critical to government function. We get what we pay for.",
      "vote": "YES"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Latam Cardano",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmWvfu4dmDYhF52Jw33PRxPKHLCXmYP7VeXGBDZ97wakKU -->\r\n# Latam Cardano\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytgsdkfznvjnwdxrzvf8x6tn4ctfukqqphsammzkfwatgsqgnpf2w`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nCompensation for the constitutional committee is absolutely necesssary. We do not think by any means this is the best compensation framework or way to do it but it is far better than conituing to expect that this highly demanding role be filled without compensation. That would a precedent that is unacceptable and dangerous for Cardano's governance quality and security.\r\nThe amounts proposed seem reasonable, in future iterations a framework linking the remuneration to the delivery of high quality work would be preferable.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Leo of the Brothership (@BrothershipPool)",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreiah3redbutvph5nhn55tlswbdvxn75ib7cdjcfody56zqkqhffdxy -->\n# Leo of the Brothership (@BrothershipPool)\n\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\n**Vote:** No\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytw5tvdxpe39l9gm3g49gl0whwd2dgu4mcf5ml5z3zfuk3s8npegv`\n\n---\n\nI am against the authority or the CC, attracting people that would like to exploit the system with rewards is a step in the wrong direction. We should be doing anything and everything in our power to deprecate the need for a CC.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Leo of the Brothership",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreiah3redbutvph5nhn55tlswbdvxn75ib7cdjcfody56zqkqhffdxy -->\r\n# Leo of the Brothership\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ytw5tvdxpe39l9gm3g49gl0whwd2dgu4mcf5ml5z3zfuk3s8npegv`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am against the authority or the CC, attracting people that would like to exploit the system with rewards is a step in the wrong direction. We should be doing anything and everything in our power to deprecate the need for a CC.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Liudmila Korovina",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreidxiossjkcmyvaow4a3tsbhgupkzvv7pn4i247rucmpr4l22edlv4 -->\r\n# Liudmila Korovina\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfpwp2v9kmgqn3trvjvs2s7yp9y60xcgxqnut6h2tw68y8svcqqxf`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm voting No. \r\nThere are no public reports on the actual workload and working hours of CC members.\r\nPayments should be proportional to contribution and transparently justified.\r\n\r\nMy ideas:\r\nReduce compensation to ₳30,000–₳60,000 per member per year.\r\nIntroduce CC activity metrics — number of proposals reviewed, time spent participating in discussions, actual reports.\r\nMaintain the principle of rewarding contribution, but with a reasonable balance and strict auditing of payments.",
      "vote": "NO"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Lourde",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreiaewmfv637yupes5chakcwn2ej2awdmxcdgpvxqfn6lhcek5k5dka -->\r\n# Lourde\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** No\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1ygtvnwgsucstvesms2hv9vc8hw3tye5zhzldmszrxqd2rxsje5c6m`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI find no value in the current form of this proposal to only compensate one third of the governance triumvirate.  The importance of the seated CC members / councils duties are recognized ; however no one voting sector should receive compensation until all sectors are agreed upon, considered and compensated. Further discussion and parameters may need consideration. I do believe in SPO, dRep, and CC compensations , but a detailed and fair package must be discussed with recorded metrics to follow to ensure fair payment and earning from treasury funds. Please raise this proposal again in future. Many of the community have willingly worked for free and do deserve a compensation package if not as a monetary yield then an access or special dispensation offering within the Blockchain and partnerchains. ***IE: Treasury investments in such Real world Yield bearing Chains, NODES, Infrastructure, and DePins, as in Midnight Network, World Mobile, IAGON, On chain DEXs or Palmyra and offer free services including but not limited to a quarterly / yearly percentage or all of the operating yield minus operating expenditures to the Voting active recorded members. An Active community investment in other communities, projects, or partnerchains to facilitate the compensations.*** ~Lourde~ Ouroborus Imperator Aeternalis",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Marius Georgescu",
      "content": "<!-- url: ipfs://bafkreignci74pt3s27huhhg33zh76mxcq3am4ls6mi7e7tx5eodzel4qgq -->\n# Marius Georgescu\n\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\n**Vote:** Yes\n**Voter ID:** `drep1yfcxs7sxzjd2ls7gjjftqmn58gg2q5fjwdcaznu0jh3uvpgceznnu`\n\n---\n\nsupport fair CC compensation to ensure unbiased and professional governance.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
    {
      "voter": "Maureen",
      "content": "<!-- url: https://most-brass-sun.quicknode-ipfs.com/ipfs/QmZbvRthBfgfmGA9vYfUkkybGDUKW21HmrB8iENGP4YuGe -->\r\n# Maureen\r\n\r\n**Proposal:** Constitutional Committee Compensation Epochs 581-653\r\n**Vote:** Yes\r\n**Voter ID:** `drep1y22hlaj8wuyygpnjy5cf96tg9tgvjrz39kxvqgv898uj9scfc55t7`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThe Constitutional Committee (CC) is not a ceremonial body. It is the core institutional safeguard of Cardano’s on-chain governance. It interprets the constitution, validates the legality and process of governance actions, arbitrates disputes, and ensures that treasury funds are released and managed according to the rules that protect the network. Without a functioning CC, there is no constitutional enforcement, no treasury execution, and no credible governance framework. The system collapses into paralysis or chaos.\r\n\r\nAt present, CC members carry real workload and legal liability. They review governance actions, verify compliance, hold and sign multisig transactions, engage in arbitration, and interface with the ecosystem during critical decision phases. These are not symbolic duties, they require time, coordination, specialized knowledge, and professional accountability. Expecting this work to be performed indefinitely on a volunteer basis is not sustainable, not fair, and not responsible. It disrespects the people doing the work and endangers the entire governance process.\r\nThis proposal seeks to fund the Constitutional Committee to ensure its members are compensated fairly for the time, expertise, and risk involved in performing this role. The requested amount represents a modest allocation, far below market rate for equivalent legal and governance oversight in any comparable ecosystem. The budget also covers administrative costs: legal setup, taxation, organizational structuring, and compliance reporting, which are unavoidable for a legally recognized governance body.\r\nFunding the CC is not a “nice to have.” It is the cost of having a legitimate, operational governance system. If the network wants treasury withdrawals, parameter changes, and constitutional processes to function properly, then the people ensuring those mechanisms are constitutional and secure must be funded. Anything else is institutional negligence.",
      "vote": "UNKNOWN"
    },
